            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#martaf">
                <p>The dwelling place of eternity in Christ. Makaria Euangelis, faithful in Christ, lived blamelessly for 20 years, 9 months, and 17 days. A most pure virgin, though she had been given in lawful marriage, she died one day before the Kalends of [...]. May your sleep be in peace.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#martaf"> 
                <p>The funerary inscription opens with a reference to the tomb as the <foreign xml:lang="grc">οἶκος αἰῶνος ἐν (Χριστ)ῷ</foreign> (cf. Orsi 1893, 390) and the name the deceased, Makaria Euangelis: <foreign xml:lang="grc">Μακαρία</foreign> in this case is a personal name and not an epithet (for the attestations of <foreign xml:lang="grc">Μακαρία</foreign> in Sicily, see LGPN IIIA 286; <foreign xml:lang="grc">Εὐαγγέλις</foreign> is not attested in the feminine form). The deceased, who died at a young age, had been given in marriage, according to Agnello's restoration of ll. 6–7, <foreign xml:lang="grc">ἁγνοτάτη νυνφευ|[θεῖσα ἐν γα]μῷ νομίμῳ</foreign>, in contrast to Orsi, who interpreted the passage as a reference to the Virgin Mary, restoring <foreign xml:lang="grc">Νυμφ<corr>ο</corr>υ|[τόκος</foreign>. Following the formula recording the day of death, the inscription concludes with the consolatory expression <foreign xml:lang="grc">εἰς εἰρήνην ἡ κοί|[μησίς σου</foreign> in ll. 9–10. This formula is frequently attested in Jewish inscriptions from Magna Graecia, including examples with the second-person singular possessive pronoun (cf. e.g., JIWE I 16, 46; II 39, 205), and is comparable to other expressions found in Sicilian inscriptions, such as <foreign xml:lang="grc">εἰς αἰῶνα μετὰ τῶν ἁγίων</foreign> (IG XIV 139, Syracuse) and <foreign xml:lang="grc">εἰς μνημόσυνον αἰώνων</foreign> (IG XIV 179, Syracuse). This expression, together with the reference to the Law at l. 7 (<foreign xml:lang="grc">νομίμῳ</foreign>), identifies the inscription as Jewish rather than Christian, despite its repeated Christian professions (cf. Ferrua 1938, 73–74; Agnello 1953, 82).</p>
            </div>
